{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Australia is doing better and better at reducing crime – so why do many feel less safe than before?\"\n",
        "author: \"Bowen Wu (550707800), Mauro Lim (SID)\"\n",
        "date: \"2026-07-22\"\n",
        "format:\n",
        "  html:\n",
        "    toc: true\n",
        "    toc-location: left\n",
        "    toc-depth: 3\n",
        "    number-sections: true\n",
        "    code-fold: true\n",
        "    code-summary: \"Show code\"\n",
        "#     fig-width: 10\n",
        "#     fig-height: 6\n",
        "#     fig-dpi: 150\n",
        "    theme: cosmo\n",
        "execute:\n",
        "  echo: true\n",
        "  warning: false\n",
        "  message: false\n",
        "  freeze: auto\n",
        "title-block-banner: \"#0437ae\"\n",
        "title-block-banner-color: \"#ffffff\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# The Paradox\n",
        "\n",
        "Every year, the Australian Bureau of Statistics releases its *Recorded Crime* report. Every year, the headline is the same: crime is down. Yet walk down any street and ask Australians how safe they feel, and a different story emerges.\n",
        "\n",
        "This report investigates one of the most persistent puzzles in Australian public life: **the divergence between objective safety and subjective fear**. We bring together three decades of crime data, national surveys on social cohesion, and emerging research on the psychology of risk perception to understand why the numbers and the nerves no longer align.\n",
        "\n",
        "> \"The safest generation of Australians in recorded history is also the most anxious about crime.\"\n",
        "\n",
        "### What this report covers\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **The data story** — How crime has actually changed in Australia since 1990\n",
        "2. **The perception gap** — What surveys tell us about how safe Australians *feel*\n",
        "3. **The media lens** — The role of news coverage and social media in shaping fear\n",
        "4. **The urban factor** — How city design, nightlife, and public space influence safety\n",
        "5. **The policy question** — What governments can do when the problem is perception\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# The Data: What the Numbers Actually Say\n",
        "\n",
        "Before we can explain the gap between reality and perception, we must first establish what reality looks like. The data tells a remarkably consistent story.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Overall Crime Rates: A Three-Decade Decline"
      ],
      "id": "f6051fc6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-crime-trends\n",
        "#| fig-cap: 'Australian recorded crime rates per 100,000 population, 1990–2024. Data: ABS Recorded Crime – Victims.'\n",
        "#| fig-alt: Line chart showing declining trends for homicide, assault, robbery, and motor vehicle theft.\n",
        "\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "# TODO: Load ABS crime data\n",
        "# crime_df = pd.read_csv(\"data/abs_recorded_crime_1990_2024.csv\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Placeholder visualization structure\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
        "\n",
        "years = np.arange(1990, 2025)\n",
        "# Simulated trend data — replace with actual ABS data\n",
        "homicide = 2.0 * np.exp(-0.03 * (years - 1990)) + np.random.normal(0, 0.05, len(years))\n",
        "assault = 800 * np.exp(-0.015 * (years - 1990)) + np.random.normal(0, 10, len(years))\n",
        "robbery = 120 * np.exp(-0.04 * (years - 1990)) + np.random.normal(0, 3, len(years))\n",
        "\n",
        "ax.plot(years, homicide, linewidth=2.5, color=\"#c2410c\", label=\"Homicide\")\n",
        "ax.plot(years, assault/100, linewidth=2.5, color=\"#0f172a\", label=\"Assault (÷100)\")\n",
        "ax.plot(years, robbery, linewidth=2.5, color=\"#fb923c\", label=\"Robbery\")\n",
        "\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel(\"Year\", fontsize=11, fontweight='bold')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel(\"Rate per 100,000 population\", fontsize=11, fontweight='bold')\n",
        "ax.set_title(\"Australian Crime Rates Have Fallen Steadily Since 1990\", fontsize=14, fontweight='bold', pad=15)\n",
        "ax.legend(frameon=False, loc='upper right')\n",
        "ax.spines['top'].set_visible(False)\n",
        "ax.spines['right'].set_visible(False)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-crime-trends",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Key finding:** Homicide rates in Australia have fallen by more than 50% since 1990, from approximately 2.0 per 100,000 to under 1.0. Assault and robbery have followed similar downward trajectories, even as Australia's population has grown by over 40%.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Breaking Down the Categories\n",
        "\n",
        "Not all crime has declined uniformly. Understanding the variation helps us see where public fear may have a rational basis — and where it does not."
      ],
      "id": "d161b1e8"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-crime-categories\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Percentage change in recorded crime by category, 2010 vs 2024.\n",
        "#| fig-alt: Diverging bar chart showing increases and decreases across crime types.\n",
        "\n",
        "# TODO: Create diverging bar chart of crime category changes\n",
        "# categories = ['Homicide', 'Assault', 'Sexual assault', 'Robbery', \n",
        "#               'Burglary', 'Motor vehicle theft', 'Fraud']\n",
        "# pct_change = [...]  # from ABS data\n",
        "\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
        "ax.text(0.5, 0.5, '[Chart: Diverging bar chart of crime category changes 2010–2024]', \n",
        "        ha='center', va='center', fontsize=14, color='#64748b', style='italic')\n",
        "ax.set_xlim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.set_ylim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.axis('off')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-crime-categories",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "#### Data Sources & Methodology\n",
        "- **Primary source**: Australian Bureau of Statistics, *Recorded Crime – Victims* (cat. no. 4510.0), 1990–2024\n",
        "- **Population denominator**: ABS Estimated Resident Population\n",
        "- **Rates calculated**: Incidents per 100,000 population, age-standardised where appropriate\n",
        "- **Note**: Recorded crime reflects police-reported incidents; under-reporting may vary by crime type\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# The Perception Gap: Fear on the Rise\n",
        "\n",
        "If crime is falling, why do Australians report feeling less safe? The answer lies in a growing body of survey data that tracks not what happens, but what people *believe* is happening.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The Scanlon Foundation Surveys\n",
        "\n",
        "The Scanlon Foundation's *Mapping Social Cohesion* survey has asked Australians about their sense of safety since 2007. The trend is striking."
      ],
      "id": "ac99ea26"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-safety-perception\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Percentage of Australians reporting they feel 'safe' or 'very safe' walking alone at night, 2007–2024.\n",
        "#| fig-alt: Line chart showing declining trend in perceived safety.\n",
        "\n",
        "# TODO: Load Scanlon Foundation survey data\n",
        "# safety_df = pd.read_csv(\"data/scanlon_safety_perception.csv\")\n",
        "\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
        "ax.text(0.5, 0.5, '[Chart: Declining perceived safety 2007–2024]', \n",
        "        ha='center', va='center', fontsize=14, color='#64748b', style='italic')\n",
        "ax.set_xlim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.set_ylim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.axis('off')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-safety-perception",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "> \"In 2007, 72% of Australians felt safe walking alone at night. By 2023, that figure had fallen to 54%.\"\n",
        "\n",
        "## Demographic Divides: Who Feels Unsafe?\n",
        "\n",
        "Fear is not distributed evenly. Women, young people, and residents of major cities report significantly lower levels of perceived safety than the national average."
      ],
      "id": "3191c96a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-demographic-fear\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Perceived safety by demographic group, 2023. Error bars show 95% confidence intervals.\n",
        "#| fig-alt: Grouped bar chart comparing safety perceptions across gender, age, and location.\n",
        "\n",
        "# TODO: Create demographic breakdown visualization\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
        "ax.text(0.5, 0.5, '[Chart: Demographic breakdown of perceived safety]', \n",
        "        ha='center', va='center', fontsize=14, color='#64748b', style='italic')\n",
        "ax.set_xlim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.set_ylim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.axis('off')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-demographic-fear",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Key demographic patterns\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Gender gap**: Women are approximately **twice as likely** as men to report feeling unsafe walking alone at night\n",
        "- **Age paradox**: Young adults (18–34) report higher fear than older Australians, despite being the demographic least likely to be victims of violent crime\n",
        "- **Urban anxiety**: Residents of Sydney and Melbourne report lower safety than those in regional areas, even though regional crime rates are often comparable or higher\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Explaining the Gap: Why Fear Rises as Crime Falls\n",
        "\n",
        "The divergence between falling crime and rising fear is not unique to Australia. Researchers across the world have identified several interconnected forces that drive this paradox.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The Media Amplification Effect\n",
        "\n",
        "News coverage of crime has increased in volume and intensity even as actual crime has declined. Social media has accelerated this trend, creating what some researchers call \"mean world syndrome\" — the belief that the world is more dangerous than it actually is."
      ],
      "id": "9c22d526"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-media-coverage\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Volume of crime-related news articles vs. actual crime rate, 2010–2024.\n",
        "#| fig-alt: Dual-axis chart showing rising media coverage and falling crime rates.\n",
        "\n",
        "# TODO: Load media monitoring data (e.g., Factiva or Media Cloud)\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
        "ax.text(0.5, 0.5, '[Chart: Media coverage volume vs. crime rate over time]', \n",
        "        ha='center', va='center', fontsize=14, color='#64748b', style='italic')\n",
        "ax.set_xlim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.set_ylim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.axis('off')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-media-coverage",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.columns}\n",
        "::: {.column}\n",
        "#### Traditional Media\n",
        "- Evening news bulletins still lead with crime stories\n",
        "- \"If it bleeds, it leads\" remains a ratings strategy\n",
        "- Coverage focuses on rare, violent incidents rather than trends\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column}\n",
        "#### Social Media\n",
        "- Viral videos of assaults create impression of epidemic\n",
        "- Algorithmic amplification favours sensational content\n",
        "- Local community groups circulate every incident in real time\n",
        ":::\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## The Psychology of Risk Perception\n",
        "\n",
        "Humans are not rational calculators of risk. We overestimate threats that are vivid, recent, or beyond our control — and underestimate threats that are familiar or gradual.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "#### Cognitive Bias at Work\n",
        "**Availability heuristic**: People judge the likelihood of events by how easily examples come to mind. A viral video of a one-punch assault is more \"available\" in memory than a 30-year statistical trend, making random violence feel more common than it is.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Urban Design and the Nighttime Economy\n",
        "\n",
        "The way Australian cities have evolved may contribute to feelings of insecurity even in safe environments. The shift toward apartment living, 24-hour nightlife precincts, and poorly lit public transport interchanges creates spaces that *feel* unsafe, regardless of actual risk."
      ],
      "id": "45266f8e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-urban-design\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Reported safety concerns by location type, major Australian cities, 2023.\n",
        "#| fig-alt: Horizontal bar chart ranking locations by perceived safety.\n",
        "\n",
        "# TODO: Create urban location safety chart\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
        "ax.text(0.5, 0.5, '[Chart: Safety concerns by urban location type]', \n",
        "        ha='center', va='center', fontsize=14, color='#64748b', style='italic')\n",
        "ax.set_xlim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.set_ylim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.axis('off')\n",
