      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### The \"eyes on the street\" hypothesis\n",
        "\n",
        "Jane Jacobs' classic theory suggests that safety comes from natural surveillance — people watching the street as part of their daily routine. As Australian cities have become more car-dependent and less mixed-use, this organic surveillance has declined in many areas.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Trust in Institutions\n",
        "\n",
        "There is evidence that fear of crime is partially a proxy for broader social anxieties. When trust in government, police, and community institutions declines, so does the sense of safety — even when objective conditions are stable or improving."
      ],
      "id": "1847866f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-institutional-trust\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Correlation between institutional trust and perceived safety, by state, 2023.\n",
        "#| fig-alt: Scatter plot showing positive correlation between trust and safety.\n",
        "\n",
        "# TODO: Create trust vs. safety scatter plot\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
        "ax.text(0.5, 0.5, '[Chart: Institutional trust vs. perceived safety by state]', \n",
        "        ha='center', va='center', fontsize=14, color='#64748b', style='italic')\n",
        "ax.set_xlim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.set_ylim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.axis('off')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-institutional-trust",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Case Studies: Three Cities, Three Stories\n",
        "\n",
        "To move from national aggregates to lived experience, we examine how the crime-perception paradox plays out in three very different Australian cities.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Sydney: The Lockout Legacy\n",
        "\n",
        "Sydney's controversial lockout laws (2014–2020) were introduced in response to high-profile alcohol-fuelled violence. They reduced assaults in the CBD and Kings Cross — but also emptied streets and shifted nightlife to less-policed areas. Did Sydneysiders feel safer?"
      ],
      "id": "cacd0cd4"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-sydney\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Sydney CBD assault rates and safety perceptions, 2010–2024.\n",
        "#| fig-alt: Dual-panel chart showing assault trends and survey responses.\n",
        "\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
        "ax.text(0.5, 0.5, '[Chart: Sydney case study — assaults vs. perceptions]', \n",
        "        ha='center', va='center', fontsize=14, color='#64748b', style='italic')\n",
        "ax.set_xlim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.set_ylim(0, 1)\n",
        "ax.axis('off')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-sydney",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Melbourne: The Post-COVID Shift\n",
        "\n",
        "Melbourne's extended lockdowns during COVID-19 changed how residents used public space. Even as the city reopened, foot traffic in the CBD remained below pre-pandemic levels — and safety perceptions shifted with it."
      ],
      "id": "f3d3fb3e"
    },
